  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "uses_without_test",
    "tranche": "T4",
    "tranche_reason": "The study develops and evaluates toxic-data fine-tuning as a jailbreak method, reporting the performance of that induced behavior. Under the rules, this does not test whether safety behavior otherwise erodes or is naturally localized.",
    "gate_note": "The reported ASR and Harm Score outcomes below are for models deliberately fine-tuned on toxic data to induce harmful responses. The paper does not test compliant-opening prefills, adversarial suffixes, decoding changes, aligned-versus-base per-token KL, or whether removing safety parameters alone removes refusal. Parameter-distribution statistics and S_score are used to select layers, not behavioral results. Cited findings are not recorded as results.",
    "test_reason": "The paper fine-tunes models on about 50,000 harmful Q&A pairs to build an efficient jailbreak, then reports its method's ASR, Harm Score and resource use against other fine-tuning methods. It never reports the original models' ASR before fine-tuning, so no row shows safety being removed relative to a baseline. The data size (50k) is far outside V1's few-to-few-hundred range. The layer-wise parameter statistics and S_score only select which layers to train; they are not a behavioural test of whether safety is localized (V5). No benign fine-tuning, per-token KL, prefill/suffix/decoding test, or deepening method is evaluated.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that lower layers are critical for generating harmful content. They find that Freeze-Front5-SFT on toxic data achieves high ASR and Harm Score with less training time and GPU memory than LoRA-based methods, and that it generalizes across the tested models and sizes.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses supervised fine-tuning on a 50,000-pair harmful dataset and selectively updates lower layers; it does not reproduce small-data fine-tuning or measure token-position KL. The procedure is intentionally designed to induce jailbreak behavior.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The number and location of trained layers change outcomes: initial-layer training outperforms later-layer training. The reported results also vary by fine-tuning method.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Fine-tuning only the first five layers on about 50k toxic pairs gives ASR 84.19% and Harm Score 4.41. This matches or exceeds LoRA-SFT over all layers at lower cost. Training the last five layers gives a lower ASR (69.27%).",
        "proposed_claim": "Large-scale harmful fine-tuning restricted to lower layers suffices to jailbreak instruct models, and lower layers are more effective to train than upper layers.",
